KMP Pattern Matching Algorithm
1. Why KMP is Needed
KMP stands for Knuth–Morris–Pratt. It is an efficient string pattern-matching algorithm used to find occurrences of a pattern PAT inside a main string STR.
The main advantage of KMP is that it does not recheck characters that have already been matched.
Example:
TEXT = ABCABCD
PATTERN = ABCD
A naive algorithm may repeatedly compare characters from the beginning of the pattern after a mismatch. KMP avoids this unnecessary comparison by preprocessing the pattern and constructing an array called LPS.
2. LPS
LPS = Longest Proper Prefix which is also a Suffix.
For every position in the pattern, lps[i] stores the length of the longest proper prefix of PAT[0...i] that is also a suffix.
Example:
PAT = A B A B C
      0 1 2 3 4
Index	Pattern prefix	LPS
0	A	0
1	AB	0
2	ABA	1
3	ABAB	2
4	ABABC	0
Therefore:
LPS = 0 0 1 2 0
Why lps[3] = 2? For ABAB, the prefix AB and suffix AB are equal. Therefore lps[3] = 2.
3. Important Idea of KMP
Suppose PAT = A B A B C and while matching, we reach A B A B and then encounter a mismatch. Instead of starting again from PAT[0], KMP uses lps[j-1] to determine where to continue.
For example, if j = 4, then lps[j-1] = lps[3] = 2, so j becomes 2. The already matched AB information is reused.
This is the main idea behind KMP.
4. KMP Algorithm
Step 1: Read the strings — Read TEXT and PAT.
Step 2: Find lengths — n = length(TEXT), m = length(PAT).
Step 3: Construct LPS array — computeLPS(PAT, m, lps).
Step 4: Initialize — i = 0 and j = 0, where i is the index of TEXT and j is the index of PAT.
Step 5: Compare — If TEXT[i] == PAT[j], increment i and j. If j == m, the pattern is found.
Step 6: On mismatch — If TEXT[i] != PAT[j] and j != 0, set j = lps[j-1]. Notice that i is not incremented. If j == 0, increment i.
5. C Program for KMP
#include <stdio.h>

/* Function to find string length */
int stringLength(char str[])
{
    int i = 0;

    while (str[i] != '\0')
    {
        i++;
    }

    return i;
}

/* Function to construct LPS array */
void computeLPS(char pattern[], int m, int lps[])
{
    int len = 0;
    int i = 1;

    lps[0] = 0;

    while (i < m)
    {
        if (pattern[i] == pattern[len])
        {
            len++;
            lps[i] = len;
            i++;
        }
        else
        {
            if (len != 0)
            {
                len = lps[len - 1];
            }
            else
            {
                lps[i] = 0;
                i++;
            }
        }
    }
}

/* KMP Pattern Matching */
int KMP(char text[], char pattern[])
{
    int n = stringLength(text);
    int m = stringLength(pattern);

    int lps[100];

    int i = 0;
    int j = 0;

    computeLPS(pattern, m, lps);

    printf("\nLPS Array: ");

    for (i = 0; i < m; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", lps[i]);
    }

    printf("\n");

    i = 0;
    j = 0;

    while (i < n)
    {
        if (text[i] == pattern[j])
        {
            i++;
            j++;
        }

        if (j == m)
        {
            return i - j;
        }
        else if (i < n && text[i] != pattern[j])
        {
            if (j != 0)
            {
                j = lps[j - 1];
            }
            else
            {
                i++;
            }
        }
    }

    return -1;
}

int main()
{
    char text[200];
    char pattern[100];

    printf("Enter Main String: ");
    scanf(" %[^\n]", text);

    printf("Enter Pattern String: ");
    scanf(" %[^\n]", pattern);

    int position = KMP(text, pattern);

    if (position != -1)
    {
        printf("Pattern found at position %d\n", position);
    }
    else
    {
        printf("Pattern does not exist in the main string.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
6. Complete Tracing Example
Consider:
TEXT = "ABABDABACDABABCABAB"
PAT = "ABABCABAB"
Step 1: Calculate LPS
Pattern:
A B A B C A B A B
0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8
LPS:
0 0 1 2 0 1 2 3 4
So:
lps = [0, 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
7. KMP Matching Trace
Initially: i = 0, j = 0
TEXT[0] = A and PAT[0] = A → Match → i = 1, j = 1
TEXT[1] = B and PAT[1] = B → Match → i = 2, j = 2
TEXT[2] = A and PAT[2] = A → Match → i = 3, j = 3
TEXT[3] = B and PAT[3] = B → Match → i = 4, j = 4
Now TEXT[4] = D and PAT[4] = C → Mismatch.
Instead of setting j = 0, KMP performs j = lps[j - 1]. Therefore j = lps[3] = 2. i remains 4.
Now TEXT[4] = D and PAT[2] = A → Mismatch again. Since j = 2, use j = lps[1] = 0.
Now TEXT[4] = D and PAT[0] = A → Mismatch and j = 0, so i is incremented to 5.
The algorithm continues and eventually finds the pattern ABABCABAB at starting position 10.
Output:
Pattern found at position 10
8. Most Important Difference: Naive vs KMP
Naive Pattern Matching	KMP Pattern Matching
On mismatch, comparison restarts from the beginning of PAT.	On mismatch, j = lps[j-1] is used.
Previously matched information is not reused.	Previously matched information is reused.
Worst-case searching time: O(nm).	Searching time: O(n).
No LPS array is required.	LPS array is required.
9. KMP Trace Formula to Remember
Case 1 – Characters match:
if (text[i] == pattern[j])
{
    i++;
    j++;
}
Case 2 – Complete pattern matched:
if (j == m)
{
    Pattern Found;
}
Case 3 – Mismatch:
if (j != 0)
{
    j = lps[j - 1];
}
else
{
    i++;
}
The key statement in KMP is: j = lps[j - 1]; This is what makes it different from the Naive/Brute Force algorithm.
10. Complexity
Algorithm	Preprocessing	Searching	Overall
Naive	—	O(nm) worst case	O(nm)
KMP	O(m)	O(n)	O(n + m)
Where:
n = length of text
m = length of pattern
Thus, KMP has linear-time pattern matching.
One-line exam definition: KMP is a string pattern-matching algorithm that uses the LPS (Longest Proper Prefix which is also Suffix) array to avoid redundant comparisons and searches for a pattern in O(n + m) time.
